WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.735
In terms of designing
content architectures,

2
00:00:03.735 --> 00:00:05.925
one of the ideas
that really help is

3
00:00:05.925 --> 00:00:08.895
using a one-by-one convolution.

4
00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:10.650
Now, you might be wondering,

5
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.970
what does a one-by-one
convolution do?

6
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.180
Isn't that just
multiplying by numbers.

7
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.890
That seems like a
funny thing to do.

8
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.690
Turns out it's not
quite like that.

9
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.620
Let's take a look. Here's
a one-by-one filter.

10
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.900
I put a number 2 there.

11
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.420
If you take this
six-by-six image,

12
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.605
six by six by one,

13
00:00:28.605 --> 00:00:31.305
and convolve it with this
one-by-one-by-one filter,

14
00:00:31.305 --> 00:00:33.945
you end up just taking the
emission multiplied by two.

15
00:00:33.945 --> 00:00:37.605
So 1, 2, 3 ends up being 2,

16
00:00:37.605 --> 00:00:40.410
4, 6, and so on.

17
00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.640
A convolution by a
one-by-one filter

18
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.350
doesn't seem
particularly useful.

19
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.645
You just multiply
it by some number.

20
00:00:47.645 --> 00:00:53.475
But that's the case of six by
six by one channel images.

21
00:00:53.475 --> 00:00:58.415
If you have a six-by-six
by 32 instead of by one,

22
00:00:58.415 --> 00:01:00.560
then a convolution with

23
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.540
a one-by-one filter can do

24
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.960
something that makes
much more sense.

25
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.850
In particular, what a
one-by-one convolution

26
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.290
will do is it will look

27
00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:13.760
at each of the 36
different positions here.

28
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.850
It will take the
element-wise product between

29
00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:21.200
32 numbers on the left and
the 32 numbers in the filter,

30
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.140
and then apply a ReLU,

31
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.280
nonlinearity to it after that.

32
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.405
To look at one of
the 36 positions,

33
00:01:29.405 --> 00:01:32.855
maybe one slice
through this volume,

34
00:01:32.855 --> 00:01:35.330
you take these 36 numbers,

35
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:40.160
multiply it by one
by one slice through

36
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.650
the volume like
that and you end up

37
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.605
with a single real number.

38
00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:49.940
Which then gets plotted in
one of the outputs like that.

39
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.415
In fact, one way to think about

40
00:01:52.415 --> 00:01:54.600
the 32 numbers you
have in this one,

41
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.620
a one by 32 filter
is as if you have

42
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:03.345
one neuron that is taking
us input 32 numbers.

43
00:02:03.345 --> 00:02:06.515
Multiplying each of
these 32 numbers

44
00:02:06.515 --> 00:02:09.455
in one slice in
the same position,

45
00:02:09.455 --> 00:02:12.350
height, and width, but these
32 different channels,

46
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.690
multiplying them by 32 weights.

47
00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.250
In applying a ReLU,

48
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.110
nonlinearity to it and then

49
00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.690
outputting the corresponding
thing over there.

50
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:28.850
More generally, if you
have not just one filter,

51
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.025
but if you have
multiple filters,

52
00:02:31.025 --> 00:02:34.205
then it's as if you
have not just one unit,

53
00:02:34.205 --> 00:02:39.920
but multiple units to taking
as input all the numbers in

54
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.380
one slice and then building
them up into an output,

55
00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.315
the 0.66 by six by
number of filters.

56
00:02:49.315 --> 00:02:52.310
One way to think about a
one-by-one convolution

57
00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.875
is that it is basically having

58
00:02:54.875 --> 00:02:58.895
a fully connected neural network

59
00:02:58.895 --> 00:03:03.655
that applies to each of the
62 different positions.

60
00:03:03.655 --> 00:03:05.630
What that fully
connected neural network

61
00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:06.650
does is it inputs

62
00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:13.150
32 numbers and outputs
number of filters, outputs.

63
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.540
I guess the payer notation,

64
00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:19.160
this is really a nc of 0 plus
1 if that's the next layer.

65
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.730
By doing those at each
of the 36 positions,

66
00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:24.170
each of the six
by six positions,

67
00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.925
you end up with
an output that is

68
00:03:25.925 --> 00:03:29.280
six-by-six by the
number of filters.

69
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.925
This can carry out

70
00:03:30.925 --> 00:03:35.890
a pretty non-trivial computation
on your input volume.

71
00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:40.765
This idea is often called
a one-by-one convolution,

72
00:03:40.765 --> 00:03:46.780
but it's sometimes also
called network in network.

73
00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:53.790
It's described in this
paper by M. Lin, Q. Chen, S. Yan.

74
00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.300
Even though the details of

75
00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.570
the architecture in
this paper on views YZ,

76
00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.985
this idea of a
one-by-one convolution

77
00:04:00.985 --> 00:04:03.250
of this sometimes called
network and network

78
00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:05.485
idea has been very
influential as

79
00:04:05.485 --> 00:04:08.080
influence many other neural
network architectures,

80
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.895
including the inception network

81
00:04:09.895 --> 00:04:11.930
which we'll see you
in the next video.

82
00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.180
But to give you an
example of where

83
00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.250
one-by-one convolution
is useful,

84
00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.565
here's something you
could do with it.

85
00:04:18.565 --> 00:04:23.165
Let's say you have a 28
by 28 by 192 volume.

86
00:04:23.165 --> 00:04:25.700
If you want to shrink
the height and width,

87
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.320
you can use a pooling layer,

88
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:28.685
so we know how to do that.

89
00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:30.230
But one of the number of

90
00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:34.195
channels has gotten too big
and you want to shrink that.

91
00:04:34.195 --> 00:04:36.700
How do you shrink it to a 28 by

92
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.250
28 by 32 dimensional volume?

93
00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.320
Well, what you can do is use,

94
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:47.705
32 filters that are one-by-one,

95
00:04:47.705 --> 00:04:50.000
and technically each
filter would be of

96
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.845
dimension one by one by 192,

97
00:04:53.845 --> 00:04:56.230
because the number of
channels in your filter

98
00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:59.275
has to match the number of
channels in your input volume.

99
00:04:59.275 --> 00:05:03.445
But you use 32 filters
and the output

100
00:05:03.445 --> 00:05:08.020
of this process will be
28 by 28 by 32 volume.

101
00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:13.030
This is a way to let your
strength and see as well.

102
00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.470
Whereas pooling layer
are used just to

103
00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.115
shrink an h and nw,

104
00:05:18.115 --> 00:05:20.410
the height and width
of these volumes.

105
00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:22.930
We'll see later how this idea of

106
00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.270
one-by-one convolutions
allows you

107
00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.120
to shrink the number of channels

108
00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.285
and therefore save on
computation in some networks.

109
00:05:31.285 --> 00:05:34.150
But of course, if you want to
keep the number of channels

110
00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.205
to the 192, that's fine too.

111
00:05:37.205 --> 00:05:39.605
The effect of a
one-by-one convolution

112
00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:41.480
is it just has nonlinearity.

113
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:42.680
It allows you to learn

114
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.390
a more complex function of

115
00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.895
your network by
adding another layer,

116
00:05:46.895 --> 00:05:49.489
the inputs 20 by 20 by 192,

117
00:05:49.489 --> 00:05:52.645
and outputs 20 by 20 by 192.

118
00:05:52.645 --> 00:05:54.530
You've now seen how

119
00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:57.095
a one-by-one
convolution operation

120
00:05:57.095 --> 00:06:00.020
is actually doing a pretty
non-trivial operation

121
00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.350
and allows you to shrink
the number of channels in

122
00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.880
your volumes or keep it the

123
00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.190
same or even increase
it if you want.

124
00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:08.960
In the next video,

125
00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.180
you see that this can be used

126
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.100
to help build up to
the inception network.

127
00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.570
Let's go onto the next video.